IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `fraud-nouns`
IMPORT `fraud-dates`
IMPORT `fraud-s15`
IMPORT `fraud-penalty`
IMPORT `fraud-s01-s05`
IMPORT `fraud-s06-s08`
IMPORT `fraud-s09`
IMPORT `fraud-s11`
IMPORT `fraud-s12-s13`
IMPORT `fraud-sch2`

-- Fraud Act 2006 (2006 c. 35), THE SEAT LAYER.
--
-- SEAT: a person who may be accused of an offence under the Fraud Act 2006 (the person suspected or
-- charged), asking what the Act makes of what they did or are alleged to have done, and the most
-- that can follow.
--
-- This layer asks the questions that person would ask.  Nothing here is new law: each question
-- takes plain inputs (yes or no, a choice from a list, a date), builds the records the layer-1
-- modules expect, and calls the SAME layer-1 rule.  A record field that no rule reads, or that
-- cannot change the result, is given a fixed placeholder with a comment saying so.
--
-- WHAT AN ANSWER MEANS.  "Am I in breach of section 2?" is answered on the facts the person states:
-- TRUE means every element of the section is made out on those facts.  It is not a prediction of a
-- verdict.  Whether a person is convicted is for a court, which finds the facts.  Words of state of
-- mind or judgment that the Act leaves undefined ("dishonestly", whether there was a legal duty to
-- disclose, whether an advantage was "in money or other property") are put to the person as facts to
-- state, never decided here.  Where the person is unsure, answering "yes" to a state-of-mind question
-- shows what follows if a court finds it, which is the cautious way to use the form.
--
-- WHO IS ON THE OTHER SIDE.  The Act names no official as a party: the prosecuting authority and the
-- court appear only as what may be done to the person (a conviction, a penalty, a question put in
-- proceedings).  The facts about other parties that the Act makes matter are asked as plain inputs:
-- who is the victim of a gain or loss is not asked (the Act does not make it matter); what the other
-- person was told (section 2), whether a legal duty to tell them existed (section 3), whether
-- payment was due (section 11).
--
-- SEATS.  One seat only, the individual who may be accused.  The company officer of section 12 is a
-- case of the same person (the director or manager who is accused because of what a company did),
-- asked in its own group.  No seat for an official.
--
-- INPUT THE QUESTIONS DO NOT ASK, BECAUSE IT CANNOT CHANGE THE ANSWER: whether a representation was
-- express or implied (section 2(4)); whether an abuse of position was an act or an omission (section
-- 4(2)); whether a gain or loss is temporary or permanent (section 5(2)(b)); whether a gain is made
-- by getting or by keeping, a loss by parting with or by not getting (section 5(3), (4)).  The Act
-- says either counts, so each is filled with a fixed placeholder.

§ `Fraud Act 2006: the questions of a person who may be accused`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Helpers that build layer-1 records from plain inputs.  They are plumbing, not law.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- What the person aimed at, from four plain answers.  Section 5(2)(b), (3) and (4) say a temporary or a
-- permanent gain or loss counts, a gain by getting or by keeping counts, and a loss by parting with or
-- by not getting counts, so the fields for those are fixed placeholders that cannot change an answer.
-- The Act's two limbs "to cause loss to another" and "to expose another to a risk of loss" (section
-- 2(1)(b)(ii), 3(b)(ii), 4(1)(c)(ii)) are asked as one question, because they have the same
-- consequence; the answer fills both fields.
GIVEN `I meant to make a gain for myself or another` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the gain would be in` IS A `Thing gained or lost`
      `I meant to cause a loss to another, or to expose another to a risk of loss` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the loss or risk would be of loss in` IS A `Thing gained or lost`
GIVETH AN `Aim`
`the aim I state` `I meant to make a gain for myself or another` `the gain would be in` `I meant to cause a loss to another, or to expose another to a risk of loss` `the loss or risk would be of loss in` MEANS
    `Aim` WITH
        `a gain for himself or another` IS
            IF `I meant to make a gain for myself or another`
            THEN JUST (`Intended gain` WITH
                          `what the gain is in` IS `the gain would be in`
                          `how it is gained` IS `by getting what one does not have`   -- placeholder: either way counts (s 5(3))
                          `how long it lasts` IS `permanent`                          -- placeholder: either counts (s 5(2)(b))
                      )
            ELSE NOTHING
        `loss to another` IS
            IF `I meant to cause a loss to another, or to expose another to a risk of loss`
            THEN JUST (`Intended loss` WITH
                          `what the loss is in` IS `the loss or risk would be of loss in`
                          `how it is lost` IS `by parting with what one has`          -- placeholder: either way counts (s 5(4))
                          `how long it lasts` IS `permanent`                          -- placeholder: either counts (s 5(2)(b))
                      )
            ELSE NOTHING
        `exposing another to a risk of loss` IS
            IF `I meant to cause a loss to another, or to expose another to a risk of loss`
            THEN JUST (`Intended loss` WITH
                          `what the loss is in` IS `the loss or risk would be of loss in`
                          `how it is lost` IS `by parting with what one has`          -- placeholder: either way counts (s 5(4))
                          `how long it lasts` IS `permanent`                          -- placeholder: either counts (s 5(2)(b))
                      )
            ELSE NOTHING

-- The statement or conduct the person made, from plain answers.  Its form (express or implied) cannot
-- change an answer (section 2(4)), so it is fixed.
GIVEN `what it was about` IS A `What a representation is about`
      `who or what I made it to` IS A `Who or what a representation was made to`
      `it was untrue` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it was misleading` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I knew that it was, or might be, untrue or misleading` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Representation`
`the representation I state` `what it was about` `who or what I made it to` `it was untrue` `it was misleading` `I knew that it was, or might be, untrue or misleading` MEANS
    `Representation` WITH
        `what it is about` IS `what it was about`
        `its form` IS `express`                       -- placeholder: express or implied both count (s 2(4))
        `who or what it was made to` IS `who or what I made it to`
        `untrue` IS `it was untrue`
        `misleading` IS `it was misleading`
        `the person making it knows that it is, or might be, untrue or misleading` IS `I knew that it was, or might be, untrue or misleading`

-- What the thing is, for sections 6 and 7.
DECLARE `What the thing is` IS ONE OF
    `an article in the ordinary sense of the word`
    `a program held in electronic form`
    `data held in electronic form`
    `none of these`

GIVEN `what the thing is` IS A `What the thing is`
GIVETH AN `Article case`
`the thing I state` `what the thing is` MEANS
    `Article case` WITH
        `an article in the ordinary sense of the word` IS `what the thing is` EQUALS `an article in the ordinary sense of the word`
        `a program held in electronic form` IS `what the thing is` EQUALS `a program held in electronic form`
        `data held in electronic form` IS `what the thing is` EQUALS `data held in electronic form`

-- Which offence the penalty questions are about: the call into the layer-1 rule of each section.
GIVEN offence IS AN `Offence in or named by this Act`
      place IS A `Place`
      `the date the offence was committed` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE `Penalty`
`the penalty the Act states for the offence I state` offence place `the date the offence was committed` MEANS
    -- NOTHING for the two names the Act uses without giving a penalty here: section 10 (blank in this
    -- text) and conspiracy to defraud (named in section 13(4); the Act creates no such offence).
    CONSIDER offence
    WHEN `fraud (section 1)` THEN `section 1(3): the penalty for fraud` place `the date the offence was committed`
    WHEN `possession etc. of articles for use in frauds (section 6)` THEN `section 6(2): the penalty for possession etc. of articles for use in frauds` place `the date the offence was committed`
    WHEN `making or supplying articles for use in frauds (section 7)` THEN `section 7(2): the penalty for making or supplying articles for use in frauds` place `the date the offence was committed`
    WHEN `participating in fraudulent business carried on by sole trader etc. (section 9)` THEN `section 9(6): the penalty for participating in fraudulent business carried on by sole trader etc.` place `the date the offence was committed`
    WHEN `obtaining services dishonestly (section 11)` THEN `section 11(3): the penalty for obtaining services dishonestly` place `the date the offence was committed`
    WHEN `participating in fraudulent business carried on by company etc.: penalty (section 10, blank in this text)` THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `conspiracy to defraud (named in section 13(4); this Act creates no such offence)` THEN NOTHING

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The questions.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§ `Fraud (sections 1 to 5)`

-- ss 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.  "Fraud" is committed in one of three ways.  Section 5 says what a "gain" or a "loss"
-- is: only a gain or loss in money or other property counts (a service, information or a favour is not
-- property), whether temporary or permanent, and whether by getting or by keeping what one has.
-- The Act does not define "dishonestly"; answer as you believe a court would find, or "yes" to see
-- what follows if it does.
@export On what I have said, am I guilty of fraud under section 1, in any of the three ways in sections 2 to 4?
GIVEN `I acted dishonestly` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you act dishonestly in what you did? The Act does not define "dishonestly"; answer yes to see what follows if a court finds that you did.
      `I meant to make a gain for myself or another` IS A BOOLEAN @desc By what you did, did you mean to make a gain for yourself or for someone else?
      `the gain would be in` IS A `Thing gained or lost` @desc What would the gain have been in? One of: money; real property; personal property; a thing in action; other intangible property; something that is neither money nor property (for example a service, information or a favour).
      `I meant to cause a loss to another, or to expose another to a risk of loss` IS A BOOLEAN @desc By what you did, did you mean to cause a loss to someone else, or to expose someone else to a risk of loss?
      `the loss or risk would be of loss in` IS A `Thing gained or lost` @desc What would the loss, or the risk of loss, have been in? One of: money; real property; personal property; a thing in action; other intangible property; something that is neither money nor property.
      `what the statement or conduct was about` IS A `What a representation is about` @desc If you made a statement or acted so as to suggest something (section 2), what was it about? One of: a fact; the law; the state of mind of the person making it; the state of mind of any other person; something other than fact, law or state of mind.
      `who or what I made it to` IS A `Who or what a representation was made to` @desc Who or what did you make or submit it to? One of: a person; a system or device designed to receive, convey or respond to communications; nobody and nothing: it was not communicated.
      `it was untrue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it untrue?
      `it was misleading` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it misleading?
      `I knew that it was, or might be, untrue or misleading` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you know that it was, or might be, untrue or misleading?
      `I failed to disclose information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you fail to disclose information to another person (section 3)?
      `I was under a legal duty to disclose it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you under a legal duty to disclose that information to that person? The Act does not say what creates such a duty.
      `I occupied a position of trust` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you occupy a position in which you were expected to safeguard, or not to act against, the financial interests of another person (section 4)?
      `I abused that position` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you abuse that position, whether by something you did or by something you failed to do?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `On what I have said, am I guilty of fraud under section 1, in any of the three ways in sections 2 to 4` IF
    `section 1(1) and (2): a person is guilty of fraud` `my false representation case` `my non-disclosure case` `my abuse of position case`
    WHERE
        `my aim` MEANS `the aim I state` `I meant to make a gain for myself or another` `the gain would be in` `I meant to cause a loss to another, or to expose another to a risk of loss` `the loss or risk would be of loss in`
        `my false representation case` MEANS `False representation case` WITH
            `the representation` IS `the representation I state` `what the statement or conduct was about` `who or what I made it to` `it was untrue` `it was misleading` `I knew that it was, or might be, untrue or misleading`
            `acts dishonestly in making it` IS `I acted dishonestly`
            `what the person intends by making it` IS `my aim`
        `my non-disclosure case` MEANS `Non-disclosure case` WITH
            `fails to disclose information to another person` IS `I failed to disclose information`
            `the information is information which he is under a legal duty to disclose` IS `I was under a legal duty to disclose it`
            `acts dishonestly in failing to disclose it` IS `I acted dishonestly`
            `what the person intends by failing to disclose it` IS `my aim`
        `my abuse of position case` MEANS `Abuse of position case` WITH
            `occupies a position in which he is expected to safeguard, or not to act against, the financial interests of another person` IS `I occupied a position of trust`
            `abuses that position` IS `I abused that position`
            `the conduct that abuses it was` IS `an act`    -- placeholder: an act or an omission both count (s 4(2))
            `acts dishonestly in abusing it` IS `I acted dishonestly`
            `what the person intends by means of the abuse` IS `my aim`

-- s 2.  Fraud by false representation: dishonestly making a false representation, intending by it a gain or a loss.
@export Am I in breach of section 2 (fraud by false representation)?
GIVEN `what the statement or conduct was about` IS A `What a representation is about` @desc What was your statement or conduct about? One of: a fact; the law; the state of mind of the person making it; the state of mind of any other person; something other than fact, law or state of mind.
      `who or what I made it to` IS A `Who or what a representation was made to` @desc Who or what did you make or submit it to? One of: a person; a system or device designed to receive, convey or respond to communications; nobody and nothing: it was not communicated.
      `it was untrue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it untrue?
      `it was misleading` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it misleading?
      `I knew that it was, or might be, untrue or misleading` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you know that it was, or might be, untrue or misleading?
      `I acted dishonestly` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you act dishonestly in making it? The Act does not define "dishonestly"; answer yes to see what follows if a court finds that you did.
      `I meant to make a gain for myself or another` IS A BOOLEAN @desc By making it, did you mean to make a gain for yourself or for someone else?
      `the gain would be in` IS A `Thing gained or lost` @desc What would the gain have been in? One of: money; real property; personal property; a thing in action; other intangible property; something that is neither money nor property (for example a service, information or a favour).
      `I meant to cause a loss to another, or to expose another to a risk of loss` IS A BOOLEAN @desc By making it, did you mean to cause a loss to someone else, or to expose someone else to a risk of loss?
      `the loss or risk would be of loss in` IS A `Thing gained or lost` @desc What would the loss, or the risk of loss, have been in? One of: money; real property; personal property; a thing in action; other intangible property; something that is neither money nor property.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I in breach of section 2 (fraud by false representation)` IF
    `section 2(1): a person is in breach of section 2` `my case`
    WHERE
        `my case` MEANS `False representation case` WITH
            `the representation` IS `the representation I state` `what the statement or conduct was about` `who or what I made it to` `it was untrue` `it was misleading` `I knew that it was, or might be, untrue or misleading`
            `acts dishonestly in making it` IS `I acted dishonestly`
            `what the person intends by making it` IS `the aim I state` `I meant to make a gain for myself or another` `the gain would be in` `I meant to cause a loss to another, or to expose another to a risk of loss` `the loss or risk would be of loss in`

-- s 3.  Fraud by failing to disclose information: dishonestly failing to disclose information one is under a legal duty to disclose.
@export Am I in breach of section 3 (fraud by failing to disclose information)?
GIVEN `I failed to disclose information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you fail to disclose information to another person?
      `I was under a legal duty to disclose it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you under a legal duty to disclose that information to that person? The Act does not say what creates such a duty.
      `I acted dishonestly` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you act dishonestly in failing to disclose it? The Act does not define "dishonestly"; answer yes to see what follows if a court finds that you did.
      `I meant to make a gain for myself or another` IS A BOOLEAN @desc By failing to disclose it, did you mean to make a gain for yourself or for someone else?
      `the gain would be in` IS A `Thing gained or lost` @desc What would the gain have been in? One of: money; real property; personal property; a thing in action; other intangible property; something that is neither money nor property (for example a service, information or a favour).
      `I meant to cause a loss to another, or to expose another to a risk of loss` IS A BOOLEAN @desc By failing to disclose it, did you mean to cause a loss to someone else, or to expose someone else to a risk of loss?
      `the loss or risk would be of loss in` IS A `Thing gained or lost` @desc What would the loss, or the risk of loss, have been in? One of: money; real property; personal property; a thing in action; other intangible property; something that is neither money nor property.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I in breach of section 3 (fraud by failing to disclose information)` IF
    `section 3: a person is in breach of section 3` `my case`
    WHERE
        `my case` MEANS `Non-disclosure case` WITH
            `fails to disclose information to another person` IS `I failed to disclose information`
            `the information is information which he is under a legal duty to disclose` IS `I was under a legal duty to disclose it`
            `acts dishonestly in failing to disclose it` IS `I acted dishonestly`
            `what the person intends by failing to disclose it` IS `the aim I state` `I meant to make a gain for myself or another` `the gain would be in` `I meant to cause a loss to another, or to expose another to a risk of loss` `the loss or risk would be of loss in`

-- s 4.  Fraud by abuse of position: occupying a position of trust, dishonestly abusing it, intending a gain or a loss.
@export Am I in breach of section 4 (fraud by abuse of position)?
GIVEN `I occupied a position of trust` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you occupy a position in which you were expected to safeguard, or not to act against, the financial interests of another person?
      `I abused that position` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you abuse that position, whether by something you did or by something you failed to do?
      `I acted dishonestly` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you act dishonestly in abusing it? The Act does not define "dishonestly"; answer yes to see what follows if a court finds that you did.
      `I meant to make a gain for myself or another` IS A BOOLEAN @desc By abusing it, did you mean to make a gain for yourself or for someone else?
      `the gain would be in` IS A `Thing gained or lost` @desc What would the gain have been in? One of: money; real property; personal property; a thing in action; other intangible property; something that is neither money nor property (for example a service, information or a favour).
      `I meant to cause a loss to another, or to expose another to a risk of loss` IS A BOOLEAN @desc By abusing it, did you mean to cause a loss to someone else, or to expose someone else to a risk of loss?
      `the loss or risk would be of loss in` IS A `Thing gained or lost` @desc What would the loss, or the risk of loss, have been in? One of: money; real property; personal property; a thing in action; other intangible property; something that is neither money nor property.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I in breach of section 4 (fraud by abuse of position)` IF
    `section 4(1): a person is in breach of section 4` `my case`
    WHERE
        `my case` MEANS `Abuse of position case` WITH
            `occupies a position in which he is expected to safeguard, or not to act against, the financial interests of another person` IS `I occupied a position of trust`
            `abuses that position` IS `I abused that position`
            `the conduct that abuses it was` IS `an act`    -- placeholder: an act or an omission both count (s 4(2))
            `acts dishonestly in abusing it` IS `I acted dishonestly`
            `what the person intends by means of the abuse` IS `the aim I state` `I meant to make a gain for myself or another` `the gain would be in` `I meant to cause a loss to another, or to expose another to a risk of loss` `the loss or risk would be of loss in`

§§ `Articles for use in frauds (sections 6 to 8)`

-- Applies only if you have, or have made, supplied or offered, a thing that may be used in a fraud.
-- s 8: "article" includes any program or data held in electronic form.
@export Am I guilty of an offence under section 6 by having this article?
GIVEN `what the thing is` IS A `What the thing is` @desc What is the thing? One of: an article in the ordinary sense of the word; a program held in electronic form; data held in electronic form; none of these.
      `I have it in my possession` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you have it in your possession?
      `it is under my control` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it under your control?
      `it is for use in the course of or in connection with any fraud` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it for use in the course of or in connection with any fraud? The Act does not say whose use or whose fraud.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I guilty of an offence under section 6 by having this article` IF
    `section 6(1): the elements of the offence are made out` `my case`
    WHERE
        `my case` MEANS `Possession case` WITH
            `the article` IS `the thing I state` `what the thing is`
            `in his possession` IS `I have it in my possession`
            `under his control` IS `it is under my control`
            `for use in the course of or in connection with any fraud` IS `it is for use in the course of or in connection with any fraud`

@export Am I guilty of an offence under section 7 by making, adapting, supplying or offering to supply this article?
GIVEN `what the thing is` IS A `What the thing is` @desc What is the thing? One of: an article in the ordinary sense of the word; a program held in electronic form; data held in electronic form; none of these.
      `what I did with it` IS A `Dealing with an article` @desc What did you do with it? One of: makes it; adapts it; supplies it; offers to supply it; does none of these.
      `I knew it was designed or adapted for use in fraud` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you know that it was designed or adapted for use in the course of or in connection with fraud?
      `I meant it to be used in a fraud` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend it to be used to commit, or assist in the commission of, fraud?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I guilty of an offence under section 7 by making, adapting, supplying or offering to supply this article` IF
    `section 7(1): the elements of the offence are made out` `my case`
    WHERE
        `my case` MEANS `Making or supplying case` WITH
            `the article` IS `the thing I state` `what the thing is`
            `what the person does with it` IS `what I did with it`
            `knowing that it is designed or adapted for use in the course of or in connection with fraud` IS `I knew it was designed or adapted for use in fraud`
            `intending it to be used to commit, or assist in the commission of, fraud` IS `I meant it to be used in a fraud`

§§ `Participating in fraudulent business (section 9)`

-- Applies only if you are a party to the carrying on of a business that is, or may be, run to defraud
-- creditors or for another fraudulent purpose.  If the business is carried on by a company, section 9
-- does not apply to it (it is dealt with by section 993 of the Companies Act 2006, which is not among
-- the sources).
DECLARE `Who carries on the business` IS ONE OF
    `a company (as defined in section 1(1) of the Companies Act 2006)`
    `a person to whom section 993 of the Companies Act 2006 applies as if the person were a company`
    `a person exempted from the application of section 993 of the Companies Act 2006`
    `a person outside the reach of section 993 of the Companies Act 2006, such as a sole trader`

@export Am I guilty of an offence under section 9 by taking part in this business?
GIVEN `I am knowingly a party to the carrying on of the business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you knowingly a party to the carrying on of the business?
      `who carries on the business` IS A `Who carries on the business` @desc Who carries on the business? One of: a company (as defined in section 1(1) of the Companies Act 2006); a person to whom section 993 of the Companies Act 2006 applies as if the person were a company; a person exempted from the application of section 993 of the Companies Act 2006; a person outside the reach of section 993 of the Companies Act 2006, such as a sole trader.
      `it is carried on with intent to defraud creditors` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the business carried on with intent to defraud creditors of any person?
      `it is carried on for another fraudulent purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the business carried on for any other fraudulent purpose, in the sense of section 993 of the Companies Act 2006?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I guilty of an offence under section 9 by taking part in this business` IF
    `section 9(1): the elements of the offence are made out` `my case`
    WHERE
        `my case` MEANS `Fraudulent business case` WITH
            `knowingly a party to the carrying on of the business` IS `I am knowingly a party to the carrying on of the business`
            `the business is carried on by a company (as defined in section 1(1) of the Companies Act 2006)` IS `who carries on the business` EQUALS `a company (as defined in section 1(1) of the Companies Act 2006)`
            `the business is carried on by a person to whom section 993 applies as if the person were a company` IS `who carries on the business` EQUALS `a person to whom section 993 of the Companies Act 2006 applies as if the person were a company`
            `the business is carried on by a person exempted from the application of section 993` IS `who carries on the business` EQUALS `a person exempted from the application of section 993 of the Companies Act 2006`
            `the business is carried on with intent to defraud creditors of any person` IS `it is carried on with intent to defraud creditors`
            `the business is carried on for any other fraudulent purpose (as in section 993)` IS `it is carried on for another fraudulent purpose`

§§ `Obtaining services dishonestly (section 11)`

-- Applies only if you obtained a service (not goods) that was offered on the basis of payment.
@export Am I guilty of an offence under section 11 by obtaining these services?
GIVEN `I obtained services for myself or another` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you obtain services for yourself or for someone else?
      `I did so by a dishonest act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you do so by a dishonest act? The Act does not define "dishonest"; answer yes to see what follows if a court finds that you did.
      `they were made available on the basis of payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the services made available on the basis that payment had been, was being or would be made for them?
      `payment for the services` IS A `Payment for services` @desc What payment was made for the services? One of: no payment was made; payment was made, but not in full; payment was made in full.
      `I knew that they were, or might be, made available on that basis` IS A BOOLEAN @desc When you obtained them, did you know that they were being made available on the basis of payment, or that they might be?
      `I meant not to pay, or not to pay in full` IS A BOOLEAN @desc When you obtained them, did you intend that payment would not be made, or would not be made in full?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I guilty of an offence under section 11 by obtaining these services` IF
    `section 11(1): the elements of the offence are made out` `my case`
    WHERE
        `my case` MEANS `Obtaining services case` WITH
            `obtains services for himself or another` IS `I obtained services for myself or another`
            `does so by a dishonest act` IS `I did so by a dishonest act`
            `the services are made available on the basis that payment has been, is being or will be made for or in respect of them` IS `they were made available on the basis of payment`
            `payment for the services` IS `payment for the services`
            -- The two knowledge limbs (i) "are being" and (ii) "might be" are asked as one question: the Act
            -- joins them with "or", so one answer fills both.
            `when he obtains them, he knows that they are being made available on that basis` IS `I knew that they were, or might be, made available on that basis`
            `when he obtains them, he knows that they might be made available on that basis` IS `I knew that they were, or might be, made available on that basis`
            `he intends that payment will not be made, or will not be made in full` IS `I meant not to pay, or not to pay in full`

§§ `What can follow if I am convicted (sections 1, 6, 7, 9 and 11; Schedule 2 paragraph 1)`

-- Applies if you are charged with, or want to know the most that can follow, one of the five offences.
-- A penalty in the Act is a CEILING: the court may impose less.  The Act states "the general limit in a
-- magistrates' court" and "the statutory maximum" without giving a figure; it states a figure only for
-- Northern Ireland and for an offence committed before 2 May 2022 (six months).
@export What is the longest prison term I can be given if I am convicted on indictment?
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN `Offence in or named by this Act` @desc Which offence? One of: fraud (section 1); possession etc. of articles for use in frauds (section 6); making or supplying articles for use in frauds (section 7); participating in fraudulent business carried on by sole trader etc. (section 9); obtaining services dishonestly (section 11); participating in fraudulent business carried on by company etc.: penalty (section 10, blank in this text); conspiracy to defraud (named in section 13(4); this Act creates no such offence).
      `where the offence was committed` IS A `Place` @desc Where was the offence committed? One of: England; Wales; Northern Ireland; Scotland; anywhere else.
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the longest prison term I can be given if I am convicted on indictment` IS
    -- The date cannot change the number of years, so a fixed placeholder is passed: the date of enactment.
    CONSIDER `the penalty the Act states for the offence I state` `the offence` `where the offence was committed` `the date of enactment of the Fraud Act 2006`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST penalty THEN JUST (penalty's `on conviction on indictment, imprisonment for a term not exceeding, in years`)

@export What is the longest prison term I can be given if I am convicted by a magistrates' court?
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN `Offence in or named by this Act` @desc Which offence? One of: fraud (section 1); possession etc. of articles for use in frauds (section 6); making or supplying articles for use in frauds (section 7); participating in fraudulent business carried on by sole trader etc. (section 9); obtaining services dishonestly (section 11); participating in fraudulent business carried on by company etc.: penalty (section 10, blank in this text); conspiracy to defraud (named in section 13(4); this Act creates no such offence).
      `where the offence was committed` IS A `Place` @desc Where was the offence committed? One of: England; Wales; Northern Ireland; Scotland; anywhere else.
      `the date the offence was committed` IS A DATE @desc On what date was the offence committed? Use ISO 8601, for example 2024-03-15.
GIVETH A MAYBE `Summary imprisonment limit`
DECIDE `What is the longest prison term I can be given if I am convicted by a magistrates' court` IS
    CONSIDER `the penalty the Act states for the offence I state` `the offence` `where the offence was committed` `the date the offence was committed`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST penalty THEN JUST (penalty's `on summary conviction, imprisonment for a term not exceeding`)

@export What is the most I can be fined if I am convicted?
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN `Offence in or named by this Act` @desc Which offence? One of: fraud (section 1); possession etc. of articles for use in frauds (section 6); making or supplying articles for use in frauds (section 7); participating in fraudulent business carried on by sole trader etc. (section 9); obtaining services dishonestly (section 11); participating in fraudulent business carried on by company etc.: penalty (section 10, blank in this text); conspiracy to defraud (named in section 13(4); this Act creates no such offence).
      `where the offence was committed` IS A `Place` @desc Where was the offence committed? One of: England; Wales; Northern Ireland; Scotland; anywhere else.
      `how I am convicted` IS A `Mode of trial` @desc How would you be convicted? One of: summary conviction; conviction on indictment.
GIVETH A MAYBE `Fine limit`
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined if I am convicted` IS
    -- The date cannot change the fine, so a fixed placeholder is passed: the date of enactment.
    CONSIDER `the penalty the Act states for the offence I state` `the offence` `where the offence was committed` `the date of enactment of the Fraud Act 2006`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST penalty THEN
        IF `how I am convicted` EQUALS `summary conviction`
        THEN JUST (penalty's `on summary conviction, a fine not exceeding`)
        ELSE JUST (penalty's `on conviction on indictment, a fine not exceeding`)

§§ `Liability of company officers (section 12)`

-- Applies only if a company (or other body corporate) you are an officer of has committed, or is
-- said to have committed, an offence under this Act.
@export If a company I am an officer of committed an offence under this Act, am I guilty of it too?
GIVEN `a body corporate committed an offence under this Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did a body corporate (a company, for example) commit an offence under the Fraud Act?
      `it is proved that it was committed with my consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it proved that the offence was committed with your consent?
      `it is proved that it was committed with my connivance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it proved that the offence was committed with your connivance?
      `my capacity` IS A `Capacity in a body corporate` @desc What was your capacity? One of: a director; a manager; a secretary; another similar officer; a person purporting to act in any such capacity; a member of a body corporate whose affairs are managed by its members; none of these.
      `my consent or connivance was in connection with my functions of management` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are a member of a body corporate whose affairs are managed by its members: was your consent or connivance in connection with your functions of management?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `If a company I am an officer of committed an offence under this Act, am I guilty of it too` IF
    `section 12(2): the person is guilty of the offence as well as the body corporate` `my case`
    WHERE
        `my case` MEANS `Officer case` WITH
            `an offence under this Act is committed by a body corporate` IS `a body corporate committed an offence under this Act`
            `it is proved that the offence was committed with the person's consent` IS `it is proved that it was committed with my consent`
            `it is proved that the offence was committed with the person's connivance` IS `it is proved that it was committed with my connivance`
            `the person's capacity` IS `my capacity`
            `the acts and defaults are in connection with his functions of management` IS `my consent or connivance was in connection with my functions of management`

§§ `Evidence (section 13)`

-- Applies only if you are asked a question, or ordered to do something, in proceedings about property
-- (recovering or administering property, a trust, an account of property), and the answer might incriminate
-- you or your spouse or civil partner of an offence under this Act or a related offence.  Section 13(1) takes
-- away the excuse; section 13(2) is the immunity that goes with it.
@export Am I excused from answering a question, or obeying an order, in proceedings about property because it might incriminate me or my spouse or civil partner?
GIVEN `the proceedings are` IS A `Kind of proceedings` @desc What are the proceedings for? One of: proceedings for the recovery or administration of any property; proceedings for the execution of a trust; proceedings for an account of any property or dealings with property; other proceedings.
      `what I am required to do` IS A `What is required of the person` @desc What are you required to do? One of: to answer a question put to him; to comply with an order made; neither.
      `who the answer might incriminate` IS A `Who may be incriminated` @desc Who might doing so incriminate? One of: the person; the person's spouse or civil partner; neither of them.
      `of what kind of offence` IS A `Kind of offence` @desc Of what kind of offence? One of: an offence under this Act; conspiracy to defraud; any other offence involving any form of fraudulent conduct or purpose; an offence of none of these kinds.
GIVETH AN `Answer under section 13(1)`
DECIDE `Am I excused from answering a question, or obeying an order, in proceedings about property because it might incriminate me or my spouse or civil partner` IS
    `section 13(1): whether the person is excused on the ground of self-incrimination` `my case`
    WHERE
        `my case` MEANS `Question or order case` WITH
            `the proceedings are` IS `the proceedings are`
            `what is required of the person` IS `what I am required to do`
            `the ground is that doing so may incriminate` IS `who the answer might incriminate`
            `of an offence of the kind` IS `of what kind of offence`

@export Can what I said in answering that question, or obeying that order, be used in evidence against me or my spouse or civil partner?
GIVEN `I said it in answering such a question or obeying such an order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you make the statement or admission in answering a question put to you, or in complying with an order made, in proceedings about property?
      `the proceedings where it is to be used are for an offence of the kind` IS A `Kind of offence` @desc What is the offence in the proceedings where it is to be used? One of: an offence under this Act; conspiracy to defraud; any other offence involving any form of fraudulent conduct or purpose; an offence of none of these kinds.
      `it is to be used against` IS AN `Against whom a statement is to be used` @desc Against whom is it to be used? One of: the person who made it; the spouse or civil partner of the person who made it; anyone else.
      `we married or became civil partners after I said it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you and your spouse or civil partner marry, or become civil partners, after you made the statement or admission?
GIVETH AN `Answer under section 13(2)`
DECIDE `Can what I said in answering that question, or obeying that order, be used in evidence against me or my spouse or civil partner` IS
    `section 13(2): whether the statement or admission is admissible in evidence` `my case`
    WHERE
        `my case` MEANS `Statement case` WITH
            `made in answering such a question or complying with such an order` IS `I said it in answering such a question or obeying such an order`
            `the proceedings in which it is to be used are for an offence of the kind` IS `the proceedings where it is to be used are for an offence of the kind`
            `it is to be used against` IS `it is to be used against`
            `they married or became civil partners after the making of the statement or admission` IS `we married or became civil partners after I said it`

§§ `Where and when the Act applies (section 15 and Schedule 2)`

-- Section 15(2) says where the Act extends.  Extent is not the same as application: the Act says
-- nothing about conduct abroad or in Scotland that a court in England and Wales or Northern Ireland
-- might try, and this form does not answer that.
@export Does the Fraud Act extend to the place where I did it?
GIVEN `where I did it` IS A `Place` @desc Where did you do it? One of: England; Wales; Northern Ireland; Scotland; anywhere else.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the Fraud Act extend to the place where I did it` IF
    `section 15(2): sections 1 to 9 and 11 to 13 extend to` `where I did it`

-- Section 15(1): the Act (except sections 15 and 16) comes into force on a day appointed by order, and
-- different days may be appointed for different purposes.  The day is not in the Act, so it is asked.
@export Was the Fraud Act in force on the day I did it?
GIVEN `an order has appointed a day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an order under section 15(1) appointed a day for the Act to come into force for the purpose in question?
      `the day appointed` IS A DATE @desc If so, what day was appointed? Use ISO 8601, for example 2007-01-15. Ignored if no day has been appointed.
      `the day I did it` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you do it? Use ISO 8601, for example 2024-03-15.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Was the Fraud Act in force on the day I did it` IF
    `section 15(1): the Act is in force for a purpose on` `the day appointed, if any` `the day I did it`
    WHERE
        `the day appointed, if any` MEANS
            IF `an order has appointed a day` THEN JUST `the day appointed` ELSE NOTHING

-- Applies only if what you did began before the Act came into force and ended after.  Schedule 1 paragraph 1
-- abolished the old deception offences (Theft Acts); Schedule 2 paragraph 3 saves liability for one partly
-- committed before then.
@export Can I still be prosecuted for an old deception offence that the Fraud Act abolished?
GIVEN `the day of the first act or event needed to prove the old offence` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the first act, omission or other event (including any result) that would have to be proved to convict you of the old offence occur? Use ISO 8601, for example 2006-12-01.
      `the day of the last act or event needed to prove the old offence` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the last such act, omission or other event occur? Use ISO 8601, for example 2007-02-01.
      `the day paragraph 1 of Schedule 1 came into force` IS A DATE @desc On what day did paragraph 1 of Schedule 1 to the Fraud Act (the abolition of the deception offences) come into force? Use ISO 8601, for example 2007-01-15.
GIVETH AN `Outcome for an old deception offence`
DECIDE `Can I still be prosecuted for an old deception offence that the Fraud Act abolished` IS
    `Schedule 2 paragraph 3(1): what becomes of liability for an old deception offence` (LIST `the day of the first act or event needed to prove the old offence`, `the day of the last act or event needed to prove the old offence`) `the day paragraph 1 of Schedule 1 came into force`
